---
// ABOUTME: Trifid cipher tool page with encrypt/decrypt form and a cube-recovering breaker.
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Michel Oosterhof
// SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CipherTool from "../components/CipherTool.astro";
import AnnealBreaker from "../components/AnnealBreaker.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Trifid cipher — encrypt, decrypt, and break online"
  description="Encrypt, decrypt, and break the Trifid cipher online, Delastelle's three-dimensional fractionating cipher, with any keyword and period. Runs in your browser."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Trifid cipher</h1>
  <p class="mb-8 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    The Trifid cipher places 27 symbols, the letters A to Z and a plus sign, in
    a 3×3×3 cube built from a keyword. Each symbol becomes three digits (layer,
    row, column); within each block of symbols (the period) the three lines of
    digits are read in turn and regrouped into triples. The output is uppercase
    letters and +; other characters are dropped.
  </p>
  <CipherTool cipher="trifid" />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    Break without the key
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-6 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    Paste Trifid ciphertext and the solver ranks the likely periods from
    symbol-pair statistics, then searches for the cube by simulated annealing in
    parallel on your processor cores. Trifid is much harder than Bifid: expect
    to need 350 letters or more and up to a minute; giving the period, if you
    know it, saves most of that time.
  </p>
  <AnnealBreaker cipher="trifid" />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    About the Trifid cipher
  </h2>
  <div class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    <p>
      Félix Delastelle published the Trifid cipher in his Traité élémentaire de
      cryptographie in 1902, as the three-dimensional extension of his Bifid
      cipher. Splitting each symbol into three coordinates instead of two
      spreads it further: each ciphertext symbol can depend on three plaintext
      symbols, which is the property later formalized as diffusion.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 27th cell needs a symbol beyond the alphabet; this tool uses +, other
      sources use a full stop or #, so check which one a puzzle expects. The
      default period of 5 matches common worked examples, including the FELIX
      MARIE DELASTELLE example. Like Bifid, Trifid resists frequency counting
      but falls to a statistical search over cubes, as the solver above shows,
      given a few hundred symbols of ciphertext.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Faq
    items={[
      {
        question: "How does the Trifid cipher work?",
        answer:
          "Each of 27 symbols has a layer, row, and column in a 3 × 3 × 3 cube built from a keyword. Within each block of symbols, the three lines of digits are read in turn and regrouped into triples, each of which names a ciphertext symbol.",
      },
      {
        question: "What is the 27th symbol in the Trifid cipher?",
        answer:
          "The cube has 27 cells and the alphabet 26 letters, so one extra symbol is needed. This tool uses +; other sources use a full stop or #.",
      },
      {
        question: "How is Trifid different from Bifid?",
        answer:
          "Bifid splits each letter into two coordinates in a 5 × 5 square, Trifid into three in a 3 × 3 × 3 cube. Each Trifid ciphertext symbol can depend on three plaintext symbols, which spreads the message's statistics further.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
